<section class="hero"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="index.html">Associate</a><span class="sep">›</span><a href="#">Module 9</a><span class="sep">›</span><span>How LLMs Work</span></div><span class="hero-eyebrow">Module 9 · Lesson 1</span><h1>How Large Language Models Work</h1><p class="hero-sub">Understand the fundamental architecture and mechanics of Large Language Models. Learn how neural networks, transformers, and attention mechanisms work together to predict text tokens. Build intuition about what happens inside the black box from input to output generation.</p></section>

<div class="objectives"><h4>Learning Objectives</h4><ul><li>Understand the transformer architecture and why it revolutionized NLP</li><li>Explain how attention mechanisms allow models to focus on relevant information</li><li>Understand tokenization and how text is converted to numerical representations</li><li>Learn the training process: next-token prediction and backpropagation</li><li>Understand decoding strategies and how models generate text sequentially</li><li>Develop intuition about model scaling and emergent capabilities</li></ul></div>

<section class="section" id="transformer-arch"><span class="section-label">Architecture</span><h2>The Transformer Architecture</h2><p>LLMs are built on the transformer architecture, introduced in 2017 in the paper "Attention is All You Need". Unlike previous approaches (RNNs, LSTMs), transformers process entire sequences in parallel, making them efficient and scalable. A transformer takes text as input, processes it through multiple layers of neural networks, and outputs probabilities for the next token.</p>

