<div class="key-concept"><strong>Key Concept:</strong> Modern LLMs like GPT are built on transformers. They're trained to predict the next token given previous tokens—a simple task that emerges into remarkable language understanding.</div>

<section class="section" id="attention"><span class="section-label">Attention Mechanism</span><h2>Understanding Attention</h2><p>Attention is the core mechanism that makes transformers powerful. It allows the model to weigh the importance of different tokens when processing each position. When predicting a token, attention computes a weighted sum over all previous tokens, focusing more on relevant ones.</p>

<p>Mathematically, attention computes three things from the input: Queries (Q), Keys (K), and Values (V). For each position, the model computes how much attention to pay to each other position using a similarity score (Q·K/√d), then uses this to create a weighted average of values.</p>

<div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">numpy</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="im">np</span>

<span class="cm"># Simplified attention mechanism visualization</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">simplified_attention</span>(<span class="va">queries</span>, <span class="va">keys</span>, <span class="va">values</span>):
    <span class="st">"""Compute attention weights and outputs."""</span>

    <span class="cm"># Compute attention scores: Q @ K^T / sqrt(d)</span>
    <span class="va">d</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">queries</span>.<span class="fn">shape</span>[<span class="op">-</span><span class="nm">1</span>]
    <span class="va">scores</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">queries</span> <span class="op">@</span> <span class="va">keys</span>.<span class="fn">T</span> <span class="op">/</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">sqrt</span>(<span class="va">d</span>)

    <span class="cm"># Apply softmax to get weights (0-1, sum to 1)</span>
    <span class="va">weights</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">exp</span>(<span class="va">scores</span>) <span class="op">/</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">exp</span>(<span class="va">scores</span>).<span class="fn">sum</span>(<span class="va">axis</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="va">keepdims</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">True</span>)

    <span class="cm"># Compute output as weighted sum of values</span>
    <span class="va">output</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">weights</span> <span class="op">@</span> <span class="va">values</span>

    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="va">output</span>, <span class="va">weights</span>

<span class="cm"># Attention visualization</span>
<span class="cm"># Input: "The cat sat on the mat"</span>
<span class="va">queries</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">randn</span>(<span class="nm">6</span>, <span class="nm">64</span>)  <span class="cm"># 6 tokens, 64-dim embeddings</span>
<span class="va">keys</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">randn</span>(<span class="nm">6</span>, <span class="nm">64</span>)
<span class="va">values</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">randn</span>(<span class="nm">6</span>, <span class="nm">64</span>)

<span class="va">output</span>, <span class="va">attention_weights</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">simplified_attention</span>(<span class="va">queries</span>, <span class="va">keys</span>, <span class="va">values</span>)

<span class="cm"># attention_weights[i,j] shows how much token i attends to token j</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Attention weights shape: {attention_weights.shape}"</span>)
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Row 3 (attending to all tokens): {attention_weights[3]}"</span>)</code></pre>

<div class="callout-box tip"><strong>Intuition:</strong> When predicting "mat" after "The cat sat on the", attention weights help the model focus more on "cat" and "on" (relevant context) than "the" (less informative).</div>

<section class="section" id="training"><span class="section-label">Learning Process</span><h2>Training: Next-Token Prediction</h2><p>LLMs learn through a simple self-supervised task: predict the next token given all previous tokens. This is called language modeling. During training, the model sees massive amounts of text and learns to minimize the difference between predicted and actual next tokens.</p>

<p>The training process uses backpropagation to update billions of parameters. Through this process, the model learns grammar, facts, reasoning patterns, and how to represent concepts—all from just the next-token prediction task.</p>

<div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="cm"># Simplified training loop for next-token prediction</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">training_step</span>(<span class="va">text_sequence</span>, <span class="va">model</span>, <span class="va">loss_fn</span>):
    <span class="st">"""One step of language model training."""</span>

    <span class="cm"># Input: all tokens except the last one</span>
    <span class="va">input_tokens</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">text_sequence</span>[:<span class="op">-</span><span class="nm">1</span>]

    <span class="cm"># Target: all tokens except the first one (shifted by 1)</span>
    <span class="va">target_tokens</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">text_sequence</span>[<span class="nm">1</span>:]

    <span class="cm"># Forward pass: predict next tokens</span>
    <span class="va">predictions</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">model</span>(<span class="va">input_tokens</span>)

    <span class="cm"># Compute loss (difference between predictions and targets)</span>
    <span class="va">loss</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">loss_fn</span>(<span class="va">predictions</span>, <span class="va">target_tokens</span>)

    <span class="cm"># Backward pass: compute gradients</span>
    <span class="va">gradients</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">loss</span>.<span class="fn">backward</span>()

    <span class="cm"># Update parameters</span>
    <span class="va">model</span>.<span class="fn">update_parameters</span>(<span class="va">gradients</span>)

    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="va">loss</span>.<span class="fn">value</span>()

<span class="cm"># On a real dataset with trillions of tokens, this process learns language understanding</span></code></pre>

<section class="section" id="inference"><span class="section-label">Text Generation</span><h2>Inference and Text Generation</h2><p>Once trained, LLMs generate text by repeatedly predicting the next token. Given a prompt, the model computes probabilities for all possible next tokens and samples from that distribution. Each new token becomes part of the context for predicting the next one.</p>

<div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="cm"># Simplified text generation process</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">generate_text</span>(<span class="va">prompt</span>, <span class="va">model</span>, <span class="va">max_tokens</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">100</span>):
    <span class="st">"""Generate text autoregressively, one token at a time."""</span>

    <span class="va">generated</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">prompt</span>

    <span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">_</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="va">max_tokens</span>):
        <span class="cm"># Get model's prediction for next token</span>
        <span class="va">logits</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">model</span>(<span class="va">generated</span>)  <span class="cm"># Output probabilities</span>

        <span class="cm"># Sample from distribution (with temperature control)</span>
        <span class="va">next_token</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">sample_from_distribution</span>(<span class="va">logits</span>, <span class="va">temperature</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.7</span>)

        <span class="cm"># Add to generated text</span>
        <span class="va">generated</span> <span class="op">+=</span> <span class="va">next_token</span>

        <span class="cm"># Stop if model generates end-of-text token</span>
        <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="va">next_token</span> <span class="op">==</span> <span class="st">"&lt;END&gt;"</span>:
            <span class="kw">break</span>

    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="va">generated</span>

<span class="cm"># Generation strategies:</span>
<span class="cm"># - Greedy: always pick highest probability (deterministic)</span>
<span class="cm"># - Top-K: sample from K most likely tokens</span>
<span class="cm"># - Top-P (nucleus): sample from tokens with cumulative prob > P</span>
<span class="cm"># - Temperature: lower = more confident/deterministic</span></code></pre>

<section class="section" id="scaling"><span class="section-label">Model Scaling</span><h2>Scaling Laws and Emergent Capabilities</h2><p>A remarkable discovery: as models get larger (more parameters, more training data, more compute), their capabilities don't just improve linearly—they improve in predictable ways following scaling laws. Even more surprising: certain abilities like few-shot learning and reasoning emerge suddenly at larger scales, without being explicitly programmed.</p>

