<section class="section" id="limitations"><span class="section-label">Understanding Limitations</span><h2>Fundamental Limitations</h2><p>Despite their power, LLMs have fundamental limitations worth understanding: they're "next-token predictors" at heart, not reasoning engines. They don't maintain persistent memory, struggle with precise arithmetic, and can confidently produce false information (hallucination).</p>

<div class="callout-box warning"><strong>Important:</strong> LLMs excel at pattern continuation and reasoning approximation, but they don't access real-time information or execute code. They predict statistically likely continuations, which sometimes aligns with truth and sometimes doesn't.</div>

<section class="section" id="summary"><span class="section-label">Summary</span><h2>Key Takeaways</h2><ul><li><strong>Transformer Architecture:</strong> Process sequences in parallel using attention. This enables efficient training of very large models.</li><li><strong>Attention Mechanism:</strong> Allows models to weight importance of different tokens. Core to understanding what makes transformers powerful.</li><li><strong>Next-Token Prediction:</strong> Simple training task that, at scale, leads to language understanding, reasoning, and factual knowledge.</li><li><strong>Autoregressive Generation:</strong> Text is generated one token at a time, with each token conditioned on previous ones.</li><li><strong>Scaling Laws:</strong> Performance improves predictably with model size. Emergent abilities appear at scale thresholds.</li><li><strong>Limitations:</strong> Next-token predictors, not reasoning systems. Subject to hallucination and knowledge cutoff.</li></ul></div>

